num_touch_reports = usb->num_touch_reports;
num_touch_reports = bt->num_touch_reports;

Reason:
These assignments trust the device-supplied `num_touch_reports` without any validation. A malicious device can set this value to a number larger than the fixed size of the `touch_reports` array (e.g., up to 255), causing the subsequent `for (i = 0; i < num_touch_reports; i++)` loop to read out-of-bounds memory (up to ~2 KiB), which may leak kernel data through evdev events.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (usb->num_touch_reports > ARRAY_SIZE(usb->touch_reports)) {
    hid_err(hdev, "DualShock4 USB input report has invalid num_touch_reports=%d\n", usb->num_touch_reports);
    return -EINVAL;
}
num_touch_reports = min_t(u8, usb->num_touch_reports, ARRAY_SIZE(usb->touch_reports));
if (bt->num_touch_reports > ARRAY_SIZE(bt->touch_reports)) {
    hid_err(hdev, "DualShock4 BT input report has invalid num_touch_reports=%d\n", bt->num_touch_reports);
    return -EINVAL;
}
num_touch_reports = min_t(u8, bt->num_touch_reports, ARRAY_SIZE(bt->touch_reports));

Reason:
The added `if` checks reject reports where `num_touch_reports` exceeds the array size, preventing any processing of clearly invalid data. Additionally, the `min_t` clamp ensures that even if the value were somehow accepted, it is bounded to the actual array size, eliminating the out-of-bounds read vulnerability.

